/**
	Shapes and exact geometric predicates over `core:math/linalg`'s vectors,
	with the same bits everywhere.

	`Aabb2` and `Aabb3` are boxes by their corners, `Circle` and `Sphere`
	discs and balls, `Plane` the points with `dot(normal, p) == offset`, and
	`Segment2`, `Segment3`, `Triangle2`, and `Triangle3` the shapes their
	points span. Every one is closed: it contains its boundary, so shapes that
	touch intersect. A box with a minimum above its maximum, or a NaN corner,
	is empty. Literals from another package name their fields, so each shape
	has a constructor: `from_min_max`, `from_corners`, `from_center_radius`,
	`from_normal_offset`, or `from_points`.

	`orient2d`, `orient3d`, `incircle`, and `insphere` return the exact sign of
	their determinant for `f32` and `f64` coordinates, as if computed in real
	arithmetic, so code built on them cannot contradict itself: three points
	are collinear or not, whichever order they are tested in. Each first
	computes the determinant in floating point with an error bound, and only a
	result within the bound, near a degenerate configuration, is recomputed
	exactly. Integers get the plain determinant, exact while it does not
	overflow, and other number types its sign as computed.

	Every result is the same on every machine, CPU level, and optimization
	level, and at compile time for whatever does not reach a square root,
	which calls a foreign procedure.
*/
// geometry-plan.md is the design this package implements, milestones 1 and 2.
@(public)
package geometry;

/**
	The sign of a predicate's determinant. What `.Positive` means is stated on
	each predicate.
*/
Sign :: enum { Negative, Zero, Positive }
